### What is linear search?

Linear search is the simplest possible search algorithm. It loops from index zero and compares each element to the target. If a match is found, it returns the index. If it reaches the end without a match, the target is absent. It makes no assumptions about the order of the data.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class LinearSearch {

    public static int search(long[] accounts, long target) {
        for (int i = 0; i < accounts.length; i++) {
            if (accounts[i] == target) {
                return i;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long[] accounts = {1080, 1001, 1050, 1025, 1100}; // unsorted is fine
        int idx = search(accounts, 1050);
        System.out.println("Found account 1050 at index: " + idx);
    }
}
```

```text
Found account 1050 at index: 2
```

### What is binary search?

Binary search works on sorted data. It maintains two pointers that bound the active range and examines the middle element. If the target is smaller, it discards the right half; if larger, it discards the left half. Each step halves the range.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class BinarySearch {

    public static int search(long[] sortedAccounts, long target) {
        int low = 0, high = sortedAccounts.length - 1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (sortedAccounts[mid] == target) return mid;
            if (sortedAccounts[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long[] sorted = {1001, 1025, 1050, 1080, 1100}; // must be sorted
        int idx = search(sorted, 1050);
        System.out.println("Found account 1050 at index: " + idx);
    }
}
```

```text
Found account 1050 at index: 2
```

### How do they compare side by side?

| Property | Linear Search | Binary Search |
|---|---|---|
| Time complexity | O(n) | O(log n) |
| Precondition | None | Data must be sorted |
| Data structures | Any (array, list, stream) | Random-access (array) |
| Comparisons for n = 1,000,000 | Up to 1,000,000 | About 20 |
| Space | O(1) | O(1) iterative |
| Stability concern | N/A | N/A |
| Best for | Small data, one-off search, unsorted data | Large sorted data, repeated lookups |

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class ComparisonDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 1_000_000;
        long[] arr = new long[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = i * 2L;
        long target = 999_998L;

        // Linear search
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        int linIdx = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (arr[i] == target) { linIdx = i; break; }
        }
        long linTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        // Binary search
        start = System.nanoTime();
        int binIdx = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, target);
        long binTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        System.out.println("Linear: index=" + linIdx + " time=" + linTime + "ns");
        System.out.println("Binary: index=" + binIdx + " time=" + binTime + "ns");
    }
}
```

```text
Linear: index=499999 time=1250000ns
Binary: index=499999 time=800ns
```

### When is linear search actually better?

Linear search wins when the data is unsorted and you search it only once. Sorting costs O(n log n), so if you sort and then binary search, the total cost is O(n log n) + O(log n), which exceeds the O(n) of a single linear scan. Linear search also works on linked lists and streams where you cannot jump to the middle.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class OneOffSearch {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Unsorted transactions: one-off search
        int[] unsorted = {500, 120, 800, 350, 200};
        int target = 350;

        // Linear: O(n) total
        for (int i = 0; i < unsorted.length; i++) {
            if (unsorted[i] == target) {
                System.out.println("Linear found at " + i);
                break;
            }
        }
        // Binary: would need sort first, O(n log n) + O(log n)
        // Not worth it for a single query
    }
}
```

```text
Linear found at 3
```

### When is binary search the clear winner?

Binary search wins when you search the same sorted collection many times. You sort once and enjoy O(log n) lookups forever. This is exactly why databases maintain sorted indexes: the index is sorted once (or incrementally maintained), and every query benefits from logarithmic lookup.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class RepeatedSearch {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long[] accounts = new long[100_000];
        for (int i = 0; i < accounts.length; i++) accounts[i] = 1000L + i;
        Arrays.sort(accounts); // sort once

        // Search 10,000 times
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        for (int q = 0; q < 10_000; q++) {
            Arrays.binarySearch(accounts, 50_000L + q);
        }
        long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - start;
        System.out.println("10,000 binary searches: " + elapsed / 1_000_000 + " ms");
    }
}
```

```text
10,000 binary searches: 2 ms
```

### What about HashMap as an alternative?

When you need only existence checks or key-value lookups and do not need ordering or range queries, a `HashMap` gives O(1) average-case time, beating both linear and binary search. But it uses more memory and does not support "find all accounts between 1000 and 2000" the way a sorted array with binary search does.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class HashMapAlternative {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<Long, String> accountMap = new HashMap<>();
        accountMap.put(1001L, "Rajesh Savings");
        accountMap.put(1025L, "Priya Current");
        accountMap.put(1050L, "Rajesh Loan");

        // O(1) average lookup
        String name = accountMap.get(1025L);
        System.out.println("Account 1025: " + name);

        // But no range query support
        System.out.println("Range query? Not with HashMap.");
    }
}
```

```text
Account 1025: Priya Current
Range query? Not with HashMap.
```

### What are the edge cases and pitfalls?

The biggest pitfall is applying binary search to unsorted data, which gives wrong results silently. Another edge case is an empty array, where both algorithms correctly return not-found. For linear search, the worst case is the target at the very end or absent. For binary search, the worst case is still only O(log n).

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class Pitfalls {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] unsorted = {50, 20, 80, 10, 40};
        // Binary search on unsorted: WRONG result
        int result = Arrays.binarySearch(unsorted, 40);
        System.out.println("Binary on unsorted: " + result + " (unreliable)");

        // Empty array: both return -1
        int[] empty = {};
        System.out.println("Linear on empty: -1");
        System.out.println("Binary on empty: " + Arrays.binarySearch(empty, 5));
    }
}
```

```text
Binary on unsorted: -3 (unreliable)
Linear on empty: -1
Binary on empty: -1
```

### How do you choose in a real banking system?

The choice depends on the access pattern:

- Small data or one-off search on unsorted data: use linear search.
- Large sorted data queried repeatedly: sort once, then binary search.
- Frequent key-value lookups without ordering: use `HashMap`.
- Frequent lookups with range queries and ordering: use `TreeMap` (balanced BST with binary search inside).

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.TreeMap;

public class TreeMapRangeQuery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeMap<Long, String> accounts = new TreeMap<>();
        accounts.put(1001L, "Rajesh");
        accounts.put(1025L, "Priya");
        accounts.put(1050L, "Pankaj");
        accounts.put(1080L, "Neha");
        accounts.put(1100L, "Amit");

        // Range query: accounts between 1020 and 1060
        System.out.println("Range [1020, 1060]: "
            + accounts.subMap(1020L, true, 1060L, true));
    }
}
```

```text
Range [1020, 1060]: {1025=Priya, 1050=Pankaj}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer matches the algorithm to the access pattern rather than always reaching for the fastest Big-O. They understand that binary search needs sorted data and that maintaining sort order has a cost. They also know that in databases, B-tree indexes are binary search applied to sorted pages, which is why creating an index speeds up queries but slows down writes. The real interview test is knowing when to use each tool, not just how to implement them.
